[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged as one issue and marked known: "the parser reads dates month
first, and our tenants write the day first." That is true for most of its reports, not all. Read by
tenant and by what each report says, #4471 holds three problems. Each has its own ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October: the parser is fixed, and the booking-v3 hint won't be fixed
because the form is retired. Nobody has looked at the one-day moves. Closing #4471 does not close
that problem.
</Callout>

## The split

Counted the way the desk's view counts: each report where it is filed, by tenant. The one-day
moves are filed under the parser and listed again on their own row.

<ProblemTable
  ticket={4471}
  issues={{
    parser: { url: 'https://github.com/CasterShell/support-kb-4471/issues/1', state: 'fixed · fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales' },
    hint: { url: 'https://github.com/CasterShell/support-kb-4471/issues/2', state: "won't fix · booking-v3 form retired" },
    oneDay: { url: 'https://github.com/CasterShell/support-kb-4471/issues/3', state: 'open · no fix planned yet' },
  }}
/>

- **parser.** The shared parser read every date month first, and every tenant on file writes the
  day first. 7 April became 4 July. Fixed: the parser now reads dd/mm for these locales.
- **hint.** The booking-v3 form printed `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. Clients typed what it
  said, and the form read the date day first. The parser fix never reached these. The form is
  retired, so no new reports come in this way.
- **oneDay.** The date moves by one day: "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got
  Monday." No day/month flip does that, so neither change above fixes it. The desk noted these
  reports in `desk-notes.json` and filed them under the parser by tenant.

## Why one ticket hid them

- **Intake tags guess.** `intakeTag` comes from the reporter's browser. The cause comes from the
  tenant: where they are and which booking form they run (`causes.ts`). Counting by tag gives the
  wrong size for the hint problem.
- **The cause table files the one-day moves under the parser.** It footnotes them, so they read as
  part of the parser bug and close with it.
- **Merging hides the rest.** One ticket with one count reads as one bug. The table the desk
  already had, for the same ticket:

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## For the desk

- Keep the one-day ticket open. A report that lands one day off after the parser fix is this
  problem, not a regression of #4471.
- Before merging a report into a big ticket, check its tenant and channel, not its tag.
- A report that does not read as a flip is not a duplicate, even with the same subject.

